I'll start by examining the files involved in each blocking finding to understand the current state before making changes.
## TASK 1 — Retry semantics

**`AttemptUsage` definition** — `crates/familiar-ai-agent/src/raw_runtime.rs:715-725`
```rust
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub struct AttemptUsage {
    pub attempt_id: AttemptId,
    pub usage: UsageCategories,
    /// Set when the attempt timed out with unknown completion: usage for
    /// this attempt is ambiguous/pending, never zero.
    pub ambiguous: bool,
    /// Provider request identity, recorded as accounting provenance when the
    /// adapter exposes one. Absent for ambiguous/timed-out attempts.
    pub provider_request_id: Option<String>,
}
```
`RunOutcome` (line 727-732) holds `pub attempts: Vec<AttemptUsage>`. There is **no field anywhere named "retries" or distinguishing a retry from a normal turn** — `ambiguous` is only about timeout/unknown-completion usage accounting, unrelated to retry counting.

**Doc comment on `run_loop`** — `raw_runtime.rs:839-843`:
```rust
/// Runs the loop to a terminal [`StopReason`]. `mint_attempt_id` is called
/// once per submission — every submission is its own globally unique
/// attempt, including a retry (the caller decides whether to retry by
/// invoking this loop again or a fresh submission; this function performs no
/// automatic provider-level retry).
```

**Where `attempts.push` happens** — three call sites, all inside the same `loop { ... }` in `run_loop` (`raw_runtime.rs:876-1030`ish):
- `raw_runtime.rs:914-919` — on wall-clock timeout (`ambiguous: true`), then `break StopReason::Timeout`.
- `raw_runtime.rs:930-935` — on `AdapterError::Ambiguous`, then `break`.
- `raw_runtime.rs:952-957` — on **every successful submission** (`ambiguous: false`), unconditionally, regardless of whether the turn was a normal tool-call round trip, an `A::PauseTurn` continuation (`raw_runtime.rs:985`, `continue`), or the final completing turn.

Confirmed: `mint_attempt_id()` is called once per loop iteration (`raw_runtime.rs:891`), i.e. once per HTTP submission to the model — normal multi-turn tool-calling conversations push one `AttemptUsage` per turn, not per actual failure/retry. Note also: `run_loop` itself never retries after `Retryable`/`NonRetryable` failures — it just `break`s (`raw_runtime.rs:940-949`). There is no internal retry-after-failure loop at all in this function; any real retry would come from a caller re-invoking `run_loop`, which none of the surveyed code currently does.

**The bug** — `crates/familiar-ai-daemon/src/local_worker_runtime.rs:266`:
```rust
let retries = outcome.attempts.len().saturating_sub(1) as u32;
```
This is inside `persist_local_telemetry` (`local_worker_runtime.rs:242-295`), feeding `LocalTelemetryRow.retries: u32` (`crates/familiar-ai-storage/src/repos/local_telemetry.rs:77`). Because `attempts.len()` counts every submission in the agentic loop (including ordinary successful tool-call round trips), a normal 5-turn task with zero failures reports `retries: 4`. This conflates "total LLM submissions in the run" with "provider-level retries."

**No existing signal distinguishes retry-count from total-attempts.** Grep for "retries" across `crates/` (excluding the bug site) found:
1. `crates/familiar-ai-llm/src/attempt.rs:260-263` — doc comment on `InferenceAdapter::submit`: "`submit` is one billable attempt; a caller that wants a retry mints a new `AttemptId` and calls `submit` again — this trait never retries internally." This is the only place "retry" is defined conceptually (a resubmission in response to failure), but no code counts it.
2. `crates/familiar-ai-review/src/coordinator.rs` — a different subsystem (PRD review/remediation cycles) with its own `review_attempts: Vec<...>` / `remediation_attempts: Vec<...>` (e.g. test `malformed_review_consumes_attempt_and_retries`, `coordinator.rs:1990-2008`, asserting `cycle.review_attempts.len() == 2`). This counts attempts consumed by malformed-response resubmission specifically, not general turns — a different, unrelated counting mechanism, not reusable as-is and not connected to `AttemptUsage`.
3. `crates/familiar-ai-storage/src/repos/local_telemetry.rs:77` — just the `retries: u32` row field itself (no computation logic there; computed at the call site in `local_worker_runtime.rs:266`).

**Conclusion for your fix**: to compute a true "retry count" (submissions attributable to failure-and-resubmission rather than normal loop turns), you need either (a) a new signal on `AttemptUsage`/`RunOutcome` marking which attempts were retries vs. ordinary turns (none exists today — `ambiguous` doesn't mean that), or (b) to stop deriving "retries" from `attempts.len() - 1` entirely, since that count is dominated by ordinary tool-call turns, not failures.

## TASK 2 — TLS / endpoint trust

**`LocalEndpointConfig`** — `crates/familiar-ai-core/src/config/registry_workers.rs:335-341`:
```rust
/// The PRD-063 endpoint profile: where the runtime is reached and how it is
/// trusted. Authentication, when required, is the generic
/// `RegistryWorkerConfig.auth_profile` BYO-Auth reference shared by every
/// worker kind — never a duplicate credential surface here.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Serialize, Deserialize, PartialEq, Eq)]
#[serde(deny_unknown_fields)]
pub struct LocalEndpointConfig {
    pub base_url: String,
    #[serde(default)]
    pub tls: bool,
}

impl LocalEndpointConfig {
    pub fn trust(&self) -> LocalEndpointTrustConfig {
        classify_local_endpoint_trust(&self.base_url)
    }
}
```

**`LocalEndpointTrustConfig`** — `registry_workers.rs:274-282`:
```rust
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, Serialize, Deserialize, PartialEq, Eq)]
#[serde(rename_all = "kebab-case")]
pub enum LocalEndpointTrustConfig {
    Loopback,
    Lan,
    Remote,
}
```

**`classify_local_endpoint_trust`** — `registry_workers.rs:284-329`. Uses a hand-rolled `extract_host` (line 284-292, manual string splitting on `"://"`, `/?#`, brackets, `:`) — **no URL crate is used anywhere in the repo** (grep for `url::Url`/`Url::parse` across `crates/` returned nothing). Classification: `localhost`/`::1`/`127.x.x.x` → Loopback; RFC1918/link-local v4, or ULA/link-local v6 → Lan; everything else (including unparsable host) → Remote.

**Critical finding: the `tls` field is never read anywhere in the codebase.** Grep for `\.tls\b|tls:` across all of `crates/` returns exactly two hits:
- `registry_workers.rs:340` — the field definition itself.
- `registry_workers.rs:927` — a test fixture literal (`tls: false`) inside `loopback_local()`.

It is not consulted by `LocalEndpointConfig::trust()`, not consulted by `validate()`, and not used to build `LocalChatConfig`/`LocalChatClient` anywhere (no production code even wires `LocalWorkerConfig`/`LocalEndpointConfig` into a `LocalChatConfig` yet — `grep -rn "LocalWorkerConfig"` only finds it in `registry_workers.rs` itself, never consumed by `familiar-ai-agent` or `familiar-ai-daemon`). The only place `LocalChatConfig`/`LocalInferenceAdapter::new` is invoked is test code in `crates/familiar-ai-agent/src/local_worker.rs:100-117`, using `LocalChatConfig::default()` (hardcoded `http://127.0.0.1:11434`, `local_runtime.rs:80-87`).

**`validate()` for `local`/`auth_profile`** — `registry_workers.rs:696-719`:
```rust
if let Some(local) = &worker.local {
    if worker.provider != LOCAL_PROVIDER {
        return Err(format!(
            "worker_registry.workers.{id} local requires provider = \"{LOCAL_PROVIDER}\""
        ));
    }
    if worker.runtime_id()? != local.runtime_kind.as_str() {
        return Err(format!(
            "worker_registry.workers.{id} local.runtime_kind must match runtime"
        ));
    }
    if local.endpoint.base_url.trim().is_empty() {
        return Err(format!(
            "worker_registry.workers.{id} local.endpoint.base_url must not be empty"
        ));
    }
    if local.endpoint.trust() != LocalEndpointTrustConfig::Loopback
        && worker.auth_profile.is_none()
    {
        return Err(format!(
            "worker_registry.workers.{id} non-loopback local endpoints require auth_profile"
        ));
    }
}
```
This requires `auth_profile` for Lan/Remote, but **never checks `tls` at all** — not that `tls == true` for non-loopback trust, and not that `base_url`'s scheme (`https://` vs `http://`) is consistent with either `tls` or the trust class. So a Remote endpoint with `base_url: "http://some-remote-host/v1"` and `tls: false` (the default) passes validation as long as `auth_profile` is set — meaning the config layer happily allows a bearer credential to be configured for transmission over plaintext HTTP to a non-loopback host.

**`LocalAuthToken` and `authorize()`** — `crates/familiar-ai-llm/src/local_runtime.rs`:
```rust
// lines 51-62
#[derive(Clone)]
pub struct LocalAuthToken(Option<String>);

impl LocalAuthToken {
    pub fn new(value: Option<String>) -> Self {
        Self(value)
    }

    fn bearer_header(&self) -> Option<String> {
        self.0.as_ref().map(|value| format!("Bearer {value}"))
    }
}
```
```rust
// lines 135-140
fn authorize(&self, builder: reqwest::RequestBuilder) -> reqwest::RequestBuilder {
    match self.token.bearer_header() {
        Some(header) => builder.header("Authorization", header),
        None => builder,
    }
}
```
`authorize()` attaches the Bearer header unconditionally whenever a token is present — it performs **no check of the request URL's scheme** (no `https` enforcement, no `tls` field awareness — `LocalChatClient`/`LocalChatConfig` don't even have a `tls` field; `LocalChatConfig` is just `{ base_url: String, request_timeout_secs: u64 }`, `local_runtime.rs:74-87`). `LocalChatClient` (`local_runtime.rs:109-140`) is constructed directly from `token: LocalAuthToken` + `config: LocalChatConfig` with no trust/scheme validation at construction time either.

**Summary of the bug 2 root cause**: `tls: bool` on `LocalEndpointConfig` is dead configuration — parsed, deserialized, and available via `worker.local.endpoint.tls`, but read by nothing: not `trust()`, not `validate()`, not any client/adapter construction path. Trust classification (`classify_local_endpoint_trust`) is derived purely from `base_url`'s host via manual string parsing (no `url::Url` anywhere in the repo), and the auth-required-for-non-loopback check in `validate()` has no accompanying check that the URL scheme is actually `https` (or that `tls` is true) before a bearer credential is allowed to be configured against it, and `authorize()` in `local_runtime.rs` sends whatever token it holds over whatever scheme `base_url` implies with zero validation of that scheme at either config-validate time or request time.
